Because $\tan\angle DBE\times\tan \angle CBE=\tan\angle DBC^2$, we have $\tan \angle CBE -\tan \angle DBE=\tan \angle DBE$, so $$\tan \angle DBE=\frac{\tan \angle CBE}{1+\tan \angle DBE}=\frac{\tan\angle ABE -\tan \angle CBE}{1+\tan \angle DBE}=h.\end{equation}$$
because $\sin \angle DBE=s=\frac{h}{\sqrt{h^2+1}}$, and $\sec \angle DBC=\sqrt{1+\frac{h^2}{s^2}}$, we easily get $$\cos \angle DBC=\frac{1}{s^2+1+\sqrt{h^2+1}}.$$
Now, $$\frac{s}{\cos \angle DBC-\cos \angle DBE}=10\implies \frac{\sqrt{h^2+1}}{\frac{1}{s^2+1+\sqrt{h^2+1}}-\frac{1+h}{\sqrt{h^2+1}}}=10\implies r=\frac{h}{1+h}.$$ Hence, $\cos \angle DBE=\frac{1+r}{1-r}$, using this we get $$r=\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}{2},$$ $r$ cannot be negative, so $$\tan \angle DBE=\frac{h}{1+h}=r\implies h=\frac{r}{1-r}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}-1}.$$
But $h=BE\sin \angle DBC$, and we want $l_{BC}=r_{\triangle ABC}=l_{AB}=l_{AC}$, so $$l_{BC}^2=l_{AC}^2=l_{AB}^2=l_{AE}\times l_{BE}=l_{AE}\times10=10l_{AE}\implies l_{AE}=l_{EC}\implies EC=4\sqrt{5}+4\). $$\text{So},l_{BC}=\frac{16}{\sqrt{5}-1}.$$
